/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Generators Generators
 * @ingroup DspCore
 * @brief Allocation-free table and segment generation into caller-owned buffers.
 *
 * Include `<DspCore_Generators.h>` or an individual family header under
 * `<DspCore/Generators/>`. All arithmetic and output use a consistent float
 * or double type. No generator allocates, resizes a LUT, clips PCM, throws,
 * or changes global floating-point mode. Integer output/conversion is deferred.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Generators_Contract Buffers and failure
 *
 * Functions return bool. Invalid parameters, nonempty null buffers, byte-count
 * overflow, or invalid ranges return false before writing output or advancing
 * noise state. The caller guarantees the stated accessible buffer extent;
 * raw pointers cannot verify its actual allocation. A null pointer is allowed
 * for zero count, with valid parameters. Empty ranges write nothing.
 *
 * Amplitudes/scales must be finite and nonnegative. Parameters and endpoints
 * must be finite. Periodic/noise generators additionally require offset +/-
 * amplitude to remain finite. Shape drive/gamma/gain must be strictly positive;
 * envelope curve may be zero. Invalid domains are rejected, never silently
 * replaced by arbitrary minimum drive or curve values.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Generators_Storage Using LUT storage
 *
 * Generators operate on contiguous public storage, independently of ownership
 * or dimensions. Allocate beforehand, then pass GetBuffer and Size for a 1D
 * table, or a row/slice pointer and width for higher dimensions. Changing
 * parameters between rows/slices builds a 2D/3D family of curves. This replaces
 * the historical virtual LUT3D::Eval and dimension-specific generator wrappers.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * DspCore::LUT::LUT3D<float> curves;
 * if (!curves.Resize(5,2,2)) { return; }
 * for (size_t z=0; z<2; ++z) {
 *     for (size_t y=0; y<2; ++y) {
 *         bool ok = DspCore::Generators::Shaping::GammaMorph(
 *             curves.GetBuffer()+(z*2+y)*5, 5,
 *             static_cast<float>(y), static_cast<float>(z+1));
 *     }
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Generators_Precision Numerical behavior
 *
 * Float calls use float math functions; double calls use double functions.
 * No platform-dependent public type substitution occurs; AVR double may have
 * the same precision as float. Stable small-argument series avoid the
 * exp/log cancellation defects in the legacy envelopes without depending on
 * expm1/log1p availability in AVR libc. Normalized shape roundoff is bounded
 * to its mathematical range; no general signal saturation is introduced.
 * Huge counts may exceed coordinate resolution; no uniqueness/throughput
 * guarantee is made beyond the selected arithmetic type's precision.
 *
 * See @ref DspCore_Numeric and @ref DspCore_Examples.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Generators_Periodic Periodic tables
 * @ingroup DspCore_Generators
 * @brief Sine, saw, square and triangle cycles without a duplicate endpoint.
 *
 * Phase is in cycles, wraps negative/large finite values into [0,1), and is
 * computed from the sample index rather than accumulated. A singleton samples
 * the supplied phase. Output is offset + amplitude * waveform(phase+i/N).
 * For @f$N>0@f$, the table coordinate and output are
 * @f[
 * p_i=\mathrm{wrap}\left(\phi+\frac{i}{N}\right), \qquad
 * y_i=o+A\,w(p_i), \qquad 0\leq i<N.
 * @f]
 * The implemented waveforms are
 * @f[
 * w_{\mathrm{sine}}(p)=\sin(2\pi p), \qquad
 * w_{\mathrm{saw}}(p)=2\mathrm{wrap}(p+\frac12)-1.
 * @f]
 * @f[
 * w_{\mathrm{square}}(p)=1 \quad (p<\frac12), \qquad
 * w_{\mathrm{square}}(p)=-1 \quad (p\geq\frac12).
 * @f]
 * @f[
 * w_{\mathrm{triangle}}(p)=2\left|2\mathrm{wrap}(p+\frac34)-1\right|-1.
 * @f]
 *
 * | Wave | Phase zero | Quarter-cycle | Half-cycle | Three-quarter-cycle |
 * | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 * | Sine | 0 | 1 | 0 | -1 |
 * | Saw | 0 | 0.5 | -1 | -0.5 |
 * | Square | 1 | 1 | -1 | -1 |
 * | Triangle | 0 | 1 | 0 | -1 |
 *
 * These conventions preserve the historical saw/triangle phase offsets.
 * The tables are naive and not band-limited. They are not streaming oscillators
 * with a sample rate or anti-aliasing. Use periodic LUT sampling with
 * LUT::Boundary::Wrap
 * for tables without duplicated endpoints. Example: Generators/Periodic.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Generators_Shaping Shaping curves
 * @ingroup DspCore_Generators
 * @brief Atan/tanh raw, normalized and positive-half curves; gamma and gamma morph.
 *
 * Atan/Tanh sample x in [-1,1]. Half variants sample [0,1]. Norm divides by
 * atan(drive) or tanh(drive), giving exact endpoints after scaling. A singleton
 * samples x=0 for every shaping family. Tiny positive normalized drive uses
 * its linear limit. Raw atan may exceed magnitude one (bounded by pi/2);
 * its scale must be <= maximum representable value / (pi/2).
 *
 * Gamma samples x^gamma on [0,1] with endpoints zero and scale. GammaMorph
 * uses exponent=pow(gain,morph); non-finite or zero derived exponents fail
 * before writing. Positive finite gain and finite morph are required.
 * Normalized results are bounded against numerical overshoot before scaling.
 * This phase provides offline curve builders, not a per-sample waveshaper.
 * Example: Generators/Shaping.
 *
 * With @f$t_i=i/(N-1)@f$ for @f$N>1@f$, full curves use
 * @f$x_i=2t_i-1@f$ and half curves use @f$x_i=t_i@f$. Their raw forms are
 * @f[
 * a(x)=\arctan(d x), \qquad h(x)=\tanh(d x).
 * @f]
 * The normalized forms divide respectively by @f$\arctan(d)@f$ and
 * @f$\tanh(d)@f$, then multiply by scale. Gamma and GammaMorph use
 * @f[
 * y_i=s\,t_i^\gamma, \qquad \gamma=g^m,
 * @f]
 * where @f$g@f$ is gain and @f$m@f$ is morph.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Generators_Envelopes Envelope segments
 * @ingroup DspCore_Generators
 * @brief Linear, exponential, logarithmic and optional LUT-shaped segments.
 *
 * Ranges are half-open [first,last), with last defaulting to total. Unlike
 * legacy code, last>total is rejected instead of clamped. Empty valid ranges
 * write nothing. Singletons write start. Longer segments include exact start
 * and end. Exponential shape=(1-exp(-curve*t))/(1-exp(-curve)); logarithmic
 * shape=log(1+curve*t)/log(1+curve). Nonnegative curve zero has a linear limit;
 * curves <= 8*epsilon also use that limit to avoid underflow/cancellation.
 *
 * LUTSegment is available when both LUT and Interpolation are enabled. Shape
 * samples must be finite in [0,1]. Tables of size >=2 must have exact zero/one
 * endpoints; intermediate samples need not be monotonic. A singleton shape
 * maps start+(end-start)*shape[0] across the entire segment, except a singleton
 * segment always writes start. This is an intentional constant-shape exception
 * to the usual endpoint rule. Empty/invalid tables fail. The entire claimed
 * output buffer must be disjoint from the logical shape storage; overlap is
 * rejected before writing. Table validation precedes output and does not
 * allocate. Output is written only within [first,last).
 * Examples: Generators/Envelopes and Generators/LUTEnvelope.
 *
 * For a range of length @f$L>1@f$, @f$t_i=i/(L-1)@f$ and every segment writes
 * @f[
 * y_i=s+(e-s)q(t_i).
 * @f]
 * The implemented shapes are
 * @f[
 * q_{\mathrm{linear}}(t)=t, \qquad
 * q_{\mathrm{exp}}(t)=\frac{1-e^{-ct}}{1-e^{-c}}, \qquad
 * q_{\mathrm{log}}(t)=\frac{\log(1+ct)}{\log(1+c)}.
 * @f]
 * At @f$c=0@f$, both nonlinear shapes take their linear limit. LUTSegment
 * substitutes @f$q(t)@f$ with the normalized table lookup @f$\ell(t)@f$.
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Generators_Noise White noise
 * @ingroup DspCore_Generators
 * @brief Reproducible xorshift32 sequence with explicit caller-owned state.
 *
 * White requires a nonzero uint32_t state reference. Zero is rejected because
 * it is an absorbing state of xorshift32. No default seed is restarted on each
 * call. Reuse the updated state for continuity across arbitrary block sizes.
 * Output is offset + amplitude*(2*state/UINT32_MAX-1), nominally in
 * offset+[-amplitude,amplitude]. Floating rounding can reach endpoints.
 * This is deterministic pseudorandom signal generation, not cryptography.
 * An empty call leaves a valid state unchanged. Example: Generators/Noise.
 *
 * Each sample applies xorshift32 to the unsigned state
 * @f[
 * s\leftarrow s\ \mathrm{xor}\ (s\ll13),\qquad
 * s\leftarrow s\ \mathrm{xor}\ (s\gg17),\qquad
 * s\leftarrow s\ \mathrm{xor}\ (s\ll5),
 * @f]
 * then maps it to the output interval as
 * @f[
 * u=\frac{s}{2^{32}-1}, \qquad y=o+A(2u-1).
 * @f]
 */
/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Generators_Windows Windows
 * @ingroup DspCore_Generators
 * @brief Symmetric Hann and Hamming coefficient tables.
 *
 * Hann=0.5-0.5*cos(2*pi*i/(N-1)). Hamming=0.54-0.46*cos(2*pi*i/(N-1)).
 * Singleton windows equal one. Hann endpoints are exactly zero; Hamming
 * endpoints are exactly 0.08 in the selected type. These are symmetric
 * windows, not the periodic N-denominator variants. Constants are local to
 * the library; no Arduino TWO_PI or include-order-dependent M_PI macro is
 * required. These functions generate coefficients; they do not multiply an
 * existing signal. Example: Generators/Windows.
 * For @f$N>1@f$ and @f$0\leq i<N@f$,
 * @f[
 * w_{\mathrm{Hann}}[i]=0.5-0.5\cos\left(\frac{2\pi i}{N-1}\right),
 * \qquad
 * w_{\mathrm{Hamming}}[i]=0.54-0.46\cos\left(\frac{2\pi i}{N-1}\right).
 * @f]
 */
